  </head>
  <body>
    <Nav />

    <div data-zoom-group data-zoom-scan>
      <section class="hero-banner">
        <img data-tilt src={img('01.webp')} alt="Two SureCall EZ-5G Signal Test app screens on a green background, showing a signal-strength check and the SureCall wordmark" />
      </section>

      <section class="marquee-section" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="marquee-row">
          <div class="marquee-track marquee-track-left">
            {[...marqueeWords, ...marqueeWords].map((w) => <span>{w}</span>)}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="marquee-row">
          <div class="marquee-track marquee-track-right">
            {[...marqueeWords, ...marqueeWords].map((w) => <span>{w}</span>)}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="marquee-row">
          <div class="marquee-track marquee-track-left">
            {[...marqueeWords, ...marqueeWords].map((w) => <span>{w}</span>)}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="marquee-row">
          <div class="marquee-track marquee-track-right">
            {[...marqueeWords, ...marqueeWords].map((w) => <span>{w}</span>)}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="marquee-phone">
          <div class="phone-frame">
            <video class="phone-video" src={`${base}images/surecall/opening.mp4`} autoplay muted loop playsinline aria-label="SureCall app opening screen with an animated signal waveform"></video>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="head-block">
        <div class="wrap">
          <div class="head-top">
            <h1 class="title">SureCall</h1>
            <div class="context-box">
              <p class="context-label">Context</p>
              <h2 class="context-heading">What is the EZ-5G Signal Test App?</h2>
              <p>SureCall partnered with Verizon to design a mobile tool that helps users identify the optimal location for installing a 5G booster. The app guides users through three core features: signal testing at a physical location, confirming successful booster installation with live strength updates, and educating users with intuitive tips about 5G UW technology and booster setup. Designed with field use in mind, this tool needed to be <strong>fast, friendly, and built for utility over complexity</strong>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <dl class="meta">
            {meta.map((m) => (
              <div>
                <dt>{m.label}</dt>
                <dd>{Array.isArray(m.value) ? m.value.map((v) => <span>{v}</span>) : m.value}</dd>
              </div>
            ))}
          </dl>

          <p class="intro">Setups for 5G boosters are often done in tough environments such as garages, basements, or rural areas with weak signal visibility. Technicians and homeowners needed an app that <em>simplified the decision-making process around placement</em> and gave immediate feedback about booster performance. Verizon's goal was to reduce support calls by <strong>empowering users with clear instructions and feedback tools</strong> within a focused, step-by-step app.</p>

          <p class="pull-quote" data-typewriter>
            <span aria-hidden="true"><span class="tw-seg" data-full="How do we help someone "></span><span class="tw-seg pull-quote-highlight" data-full="confidently choose where and how to install"></span><span class="tw-seg" data-full=" a signal booster?"></span></span>
            <span class="sr-only">How do we help someone confidently choose where and how to install a signal booster?</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="stat-band">
        <div class="location-block">
          <p class="location-label">Project Location</p>
          <p class="location-name">Silicon Valley, California</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="dark-block">
        <div class="wrap">
          <div class="challenge-head">
            <span class="quirky quirky-blue">Signal Data</span>
            <h2>The Challenge</h2>
          </div>
          <ol class="challenge-grid">
            {challenges.map((c, i) => (
              <li class:list={['challenge-card', 'challenge-card-blue', { 'is-active': i === 0 }]} data-tilt>
                <span class="challenge-num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <p>{c}</p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="white-block">
        <div class="wrap">
          <h2>What We Heard</h2>
          <p>I didn't collect direct user research, but I was given a clear brief from the SureCall and Verizon project managers: most clients have no mental model for what a good or bad dBm value means, <span class="callout-phrase">people want to "see it work" in real time to build trust</span>, and technical instructions need to be <strong>skimmable with optional depth</strong>. From that brief, we built two personas to keep users' goals and pain points in view.</p>
          <div class="image-row image-row-personas">
            <img data-tilt src={img('03.webp')} alt="User persona: Sarah Scott, 38, a remote marketing worker in Wisconsin installing her first signal booster, with her goals, pain points, and needs from the app" />
            <img data-tilt src={img('04.webp')} alt="User persona: Jamal Ortiz, 29, a field technician in Texas who installs boosters professionally, with his goals, pain points, and needs from the app" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="white-block">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow-label">Signal Page Iteration</p>
          <p>For the signal page, we had to determine <strong>what information earned a place in the visual hierarchy versus what could be cut</strong>. This page is meant to test the signal of the booster once the user has installed it already.</p>
          <div class="image-row-single image-row-single-wide">
            <img data-tilt src={img('09.webp')} alt="Two iteration options for the signal-test page: Option 1 with Test Again and Learn More buttons, and Option 2 showing an automatic rescan with a Close button" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-band">
        <div class="feature-media">
          <img data-tilt src={img('13.webp')} alt="Two SureCall screens showing the signal search state and the color-coded Great signal-strength result" />
        </div>
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h2 class="feature-heading">Signal Strength, Translated</h2>
          <p>A color-coded meter with clear labels like "Good" and "Great" turns a technical value like -80 dBm into <em>something intuitive at a glance</em>. Large tap targets and step-by-step screens keep the whole thing usable one-handed, in the field. Rather than relying on a "Test Again" button, the app <strong>automatically rescans the signal after installation</strong>, cutting out a manual step. A "Close" button guides users straight back to the home screen instead of dead-ending on a results page, and the RSRP value stays visible alongside the plain-language label for anyone who wants the technical reading too.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="dark-block">
        <div class="wrap">
          <div class="challenge-head">
            <span class="quirky quirky-sage">Plain-Language Education</span>
            <h2>The Challenge</h2>
          </div>
          <ol class="challenge-grid">
            {challenges.map((c, i) => (
              <li class:list={['challenge-card', 'challenge-card-sage', { 'is-active': i === 1 }]} data-tilt>
                <span class="challenge-num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <p>{c}</p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="white-block">
        <div class="wrap">
          <div class="image-row-single image-row-single-wide">
            <img data-tilt src={img('08.webp')} alt="Three iteration options for explaining 5G C-Band and UW technology: plain information with no aesthetic, relevant information in a gradient bubble, and relevant information with color-coded signal specifics" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-band feature-band-reverse">
        <div class="feature-media">
          <img data-tilt src={img('14.webp')} alt="Three SureCall screens guiding a user to move near a window, checking 5G UW strength, and offering booster tips" />
        </div>
        <div class="feature-text">
          <h2 class="feature-heading">Confidence During Setup</h2>
          <p>Instant testing tools close the feedback loop within seconds, so users feel reassured instead of second-guessing whether the install actually worked — no advanced understanding required. Boosters are often installed in tough spots — garages, basements, rural areas with weak signal to begin with, a standing edge case for the interface — so <strong>a clear pass-or-fail moment mattered more than a polished readout</strong>. Mid-fidelity iterations leaned on the <em>aesthetic-usability effect</em>: a balanced, uncluttered layout that felt trustworthy before a user read a single word on the screen.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="dark-block">
        <div class="wrap">
          <div class="challenge-head">
            <span class="quirky quirky-coral">Low-Friction Field Use</span>
            <h2>The Challenge</h2>
          </div>
          <ol class="challenge-grid">
            {challenges.map((c, i) => (
              <li class:list={['challenge-card', 'challenge-card-coral', { 'is-active': i === 2 }]} data-tilt>
                <span class="challenge-num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
